ghout the home, as well as expert technology support and network security services. It also provides various residential video services from basic video service to digital services with access to hundreds of channels. In addition, the company offers Sparklight TV, an IPTV video service and a cloud-based digital video recorder (DVR) service that allows customers to stream video channels from the cloud through a new app on supported devices, such as the Amazon Firestick, Apple TV, and Android-based smart televisions that does not require the use of a set-top box. Further, it provides traditional telecommunications services; residential voice services; fiber optic-based products include dark fiber, E-Line, E-Lan and E-Access ethernet services; and network-to-network interface connections. Additionally, it provides data, voice, and video products to business customers, including small to mid-markets, enterprises, and wholesale and carrier customers. Cable One, Inc. was incorporated in 1980 and is headquartered in Phoenix, Arizona.","ceo_bio":"Mr. James A. Holanda | CEO & Director | Age 60","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 2K people. Revenue per employee of $579K ranks in the below the sector median among Communication Services peers, with revenue growing 6%/yr across its filed history. Each employee generates $107K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $16K per employee reflects an ownership culture. Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (6/10).","financial_summary":["Cable One, Inc. is a micro-cap communication services business with a $17M market cap on $1.5B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at -4% over the past three years and +6% annually across the full decade, and a shrinking top line is the hardest headwind any business can overcome. Operating margins are contracting by -3.4pp, the first warning that competitive pressure is eroding the franchise.","Returns on capital run at 8% against a 7% cost of capital, which means the business barely earns its keep economically.","The business throws off $278M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 19 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders.","On the balance sheet, debt is heavy at 18.8\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and leverage like that is what turns a bad year into a permanent loss, and thin -1.6\u00d7 interest coverage leaves no room for a bad year.","Insiders hold 7.1% of the shares, and profit capture is only 0.0\u00d7 the fair share, suggesting no structural edge inside its industry."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Operating margins contracting -3.4pp YoY \u2014 competitive pressure is eroding the franchise","sev":"red","cat":"trend"},{"text":"Altman Z in the distress zone (0.4) \u2014 the kind of balance-sheet risk that can make a loss permanent","sev":"red","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Very elevated short interest (38.0% of float) \u2014 sophisticated money is betting against the business","sev":"red","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Payout ratio of 457% leaves essentially no coverage if earnings wobble","sev":"red","cat":"dividend"},{"text":"News sentiment sharply negative (-0.30) across 12 recent articles \u2014 the narrative has turned","sev":"red","cat":"news"},{"text":"Operating margins 22% below sector median \u2014 a cost disadvantage the moat cannot hide","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Fragmented industry with below-average profit capture \u2014 no moat to protect margins","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Piotroski score 4/9 \u2014 below the median for a healthy business","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"AI-generated content commoditisation \u2014 as creation costs collapse toward zero, content libraries lose pricing power and differentiation narrows.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Trading at a 99%